{"post": {"ID": 462, "post_title": "SEO Mistakes to Avoid: 12 Common SEO Mistakes and How to Fix Them", "post_excerpt": "Twelve common SEO mistakes in the order to fix them, with how to spot each one, the fix, data from the 2025 Web Almanac and SE Ranking audits, a free 60-minute audit and a worked example.", "post_author": "3", "post_date": "2025-03-04 12:50:06", "post_modified": "2026-11-15 19:39:42", "post_content_b64": ""}, "meta": {"_thumbnail_id": "566", "_yoast_wpseo_title": "SEO Mistakes to Avoid: 12 Common Mistakes | Reinforce Lab", "_yoast_wpseo_metadesc": "SEO mistakes to avoid: 12 common SEO errors in fix order, on-page and technical checklists, hiring red flags and a free 60-minute audit you can run today.", "rl_updated": "2026-11-16", "_rl_carried": "a:3:{s:7:\"prod_id\";i:228658;s:4:\"from\";s:47:\"https://reinforcelab.com/seo-mistakes-to-avoid/\";s:8:\"decision\";s:5:\"D-178\";}", "rl_type": "checklist", "_yoast_wpseo_primary_category": "12", "_yoast_wpseo_focuskw": "seo mistakes", "rl_answer": "The most common SEO mistakes are basic: a stray noindex tag, content search engines cannot see, duplicate or missing titles and headings, thin copycat pages, weak internal links and slow pages. Fix access and indexing first, then relevance and content, then structure, speed and polish, and measure the result in Search Console and in leads.", "rl_faqs": "What are the most common SEO mistakes? | Blocking or noindexing pages by accident, content that search engines cannot see, duplicate or missing titles and headings, thin or copycat content, weak internal links, keyword stuffing, slow pages, images without descriptions and markup that does not match the page.\nWhat is the biggest SEO mistake? | An unintended noindex tag or robots block, because nothing else helps while a page cannot be indexed. Check Search Console's Page Indexing report for pages excluded by noindex or blocked by robots.txt.\nHow do I find SEO mistakes on my website? | Use Search Console for indexing, URL Inspection for rendering, a crawler for titles, headings, status codes and internal links, PageSpeed Insights or the Core Web Vitals report for speed, and the Rich Results Test for markup.\nHow common are missing meta descriptions and H1 tags? | The 2025 Web Almanac found meta descriptions on 67.7% of desktop pages and an H1 on 71%. SE Ranking's audited sites showed missing meta descriptions on 65.38% and missing H1s on 54.67%. The two data sets measure different things.\nDoes keyword stuffing still hurt SEO? | Yes. Google's spam policies list keyword stuffing as a manipulation technique. Use the keyword where it fits and write for the reader's question.\nWhat are the Core Web Vitals thresholds? | Google's web.dev recommends LCP within 2.5 seconds, INP of 200 milliseconds and CLS of 0.1, measured at the 75th percentile of page loads and segmented by mobile and desktop.\nDo duplicate titles matter? | They make pages hard to tell apart in results and can signal thin or duplicated content. SE Ranking found duplicate titles on 53.69% of audited sites. Give each page a distinct, accurate title.\nIs AI-written content an SEO mistake? | Not by itself. Google says appropriate AI use is not against its guidelines. Generating many pages without adding value for users is listed as scaled content abuse. Add original value and check every fact.\nHow often should I run an SEO audit? | Run a short check monthly and a fuller audit each quarter and after any redesign, migration or plugin change, since a stray tag or setting can undo months of work.\nDo I need paid tools to avoid SEO mistakes? | Not to start. Search Console, URL Inspection, PageSpeed Insights, the Rich Results Test and any basic crawler cover most checks. Paid tools help at scale.\nWhat are common on-page SEO mistakes? | Missing or duplicate titles and descriptions, a messy heading structure, thin or copycat content, keyword stuffing, pages that compete with each other, weak internal links and images with no description.\nWhat are common technical SEO mistakes? | An accidental noindex tag or robots block, content that crawlers cannot see, duplicate pages with mixed canonical signals, slow or unstable pages and markup that does not match the page.\nWhat mistakes should I avoid when hiring an SEO company? | Do not accept ranking guarantees, secrecy or cold emails. Ask for examples, a plan and how success is measured, start with read-only access to Search Console, and never agree to link schemes.", "rl_sources": "HTTP Archive: The 2025 Web Almanac, SEO chapter | https://almanac.httparchive.org/en/2025/seo\nSE Ranking: 33 technical SEO issues affecting most websites (March 2025) | https://seranking.com/blog/seo-issues/\nweb.dev: Web Vitals, essential metrics for a healthy site | https://web.dev/articles/vitals\nGoogle Search Central: Guide to optimizing for generative AI features on Google Search | https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/ai-optimization-guide\nGoogle Search Central: Spam policies for Google web search | https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies\nVercel: The rise of the AI crawler (December 2024) | https://vercel.com/blog/the-rise-of-the-ai-crawler\nGoogle Search Central: AI features and your website | https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/ai-features\nSearch Engine Land: What is on-page SEO? Key elements and best practices | https://searchengineland.com/guide/what-is-on-page-seo\nGoogle Search Central: Do you need an SEO? Tips for hiring an SEO | https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/do-i-need-seo", "rl_takeaways": "Fix access and indexing first. A stray noindex tag or robots block stops everything else from working.\nThe 2025 Web Almanac found titles on 98.6% of desktop pages but meta descriptions on 67.7%, an H1 on 71% and canonical tags on 68%.\nSE Ranking's audits found images without descriptions on 74.43% of sites and duplicate titles on 53.69%. These are audited sites, not all sites.\nGoogle's recommended Core Web Vitals targets at the 75th percentile are LCP 2.5 seconds, INP 200 milliseconds and CLS 0.1.\nThin or copycat content is a content problem, not a tag problem. Add first-hand evidence, examples or data.\nPick one preferred URL for each page and avoid mixed canonical, sitemap and robots signals.\nRun a free 60-minute audit with Search Console, a crawler and PageSpeed Insights, and keep it as a habit.", "rl_g_glossary": "Noindex | A tag that tells search engines not to show a page in results.\nCanonical tag | A hint that names the preferred URL when several pages have the same content.\nCrawl | When a program visits your pages to find content and links.\nOrphan page | A page that no other page on your site links to.\nCore Web Vitals | Google's measures of loading speed, responsiveness and visual stability.\nInformation gain | New, useful material that a page adds beyond what others already say.", "rl_g_cta_service": "services/technical-seo-services", "rl_g_cta_head": "Want a technical SEO check of your site?", "rl_g_cta_sub": "Reinforce Lab starts with a free diagnostic of your Google and AI search visibility and a 90-day plan.", "rl_g_cta_button": "See technical SEO"}}